WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.436 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:07.040
-Hey, everybody, welcome
once again to "Corrections,"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.142
and we're going
to get right into it.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:14.547
We did a joke about
how Big Bird

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:17.617
got the COVID vaccine,
and the punch line

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:21.153
was he's now the quarterback
for the Green Bay Packers,

00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:24.457
and we then showed
a graphic of Big Bird

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:25.792
wearing a football helmet.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.261
Football helmets are for safety.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:31.397
That is the role they play.

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:34.099
And some of you
correctly pointed out

00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:35.701
it would not be that safe

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:40.340
if the bar was between the top
and bottom of your beak.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.645
I called Big Bird a Muppet,

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.313
and some people said
he's not a Muppet,

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:51.383
but I'm going to die
on that hill.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:53.987
I think he's a Muppet.

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:55.688
Prove I'm wrong.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.594
We had another joke about
how Oscar the Grouch

00:01:02.594 --> 00:01:07.666
got the Hep C vaccine.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:10.135
And I was told that
while there is a vaccine

00:01:10.135 --> 00:01:11.470
for hepatitis A and B,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.807
there is not, to this point,
a hepatitis C vaccine.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:17.510
They have been trying
to develop one,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.212
but I guess
they can't figure out

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.881
how to get
the tracking chip to work.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.550
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.454
And so the FDA won't approve it
because they're like,

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:31.458
"If we can't track the people
who get it, what's the point?"

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:38.631
I try very hard not to
name-drop during "Corrections."

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:42.534
And this is not a correction.
This is just a celebrity story.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.203
I met Big Bird.

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:47.239
Caroll Spinney,
the original Big Bird.

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:48.241
Rest in peace.

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:51.177
Caroll Spinney came to SNL.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.911
Did an Update features
with Caroll Spinney

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:57.817
and the most fascinating part
about the whole thing

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:02.354
was how Caroll Spinney
reads his lines,

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:04.524
because he can't see
the cue cards

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:09.729
because he's in
a giant bird costume.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:15.068
And he had a pen light
that he held in his mouth

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:19.605
and like a tiny clipboard
where he was reading it.

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:24.844
And at one point, live,
I heard the clipboard drop.

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:27.546
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.282
And later, Erik Kenward,

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.083
who's a writer
and producer at "SNL,"

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:33.253
said that watching
the whole thing was like

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:36.856
watching someone operate
a Civil War submarine.

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:39.993
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.128
This one, sometimes
this happens on the show.

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:46.199
I said Necco Wafers,
people told me Necco Wafers

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:49.002
were discontinued,
I corrected myself,

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:52.238
and then a slew of people
said Necco Wafers

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.975
were discontinued
for two years

00:02:55.975 --> 00:03:01.314
and then a different company
bought Necco

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.483
and is now -- you can get
Necco Wafers.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.784
I also did not know this --

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:08.588
Necco stands for New England
Confectionary Company.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:11.390
And they were bought
by Spangler.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.858
Which stands for
Southern Pennsylvania

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:15.061
Artisanal Nougat,
Gums, and Licorices.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:23.035
So, Necco Wafers
are still available.

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:26.372
That delicious --
the delicious taste

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.441
of the body of candy Christ.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:32.111
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:33.613
Came back.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.248
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.819
I said...

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.455
I said "anime cartoon."

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.191
I was told that's redundant.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.193
Anime and cartoon mean
the same thing.

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.627
It would be like saying

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:50.496
"a nerdy anime fan."

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:57.970
Oh, I pronounced
"Yoseph" Goebbels,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.473
"Joseph" Goebbels.

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:03.676
I apologized on behalf
of getting that wrong

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:08.981
to the entire Goebbels
family, and someone

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:11.183
wrote an interesting fact
that Joseph Goebbels

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:13.285
doesn't have any family left
to apologize to.

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.154
His, um...

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:20.126
At the end of the war...

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.396
Oof.

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.598
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:29.268
That is, uh...

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:31.437
That is not, uh, for this.

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:33.273
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:43.883
We had been talking about
the fairy tale

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:46.685
"The Elves and the Shoemaker."
You know, the classic fairy tale

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:49.655
where the shoemaker goes to bed
and the elves make the shoes.

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:55.828
We were talking about it
in reference to the fairy tale

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.197
"Shoemaker and the Elves,"
which is when the elves

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.800
who work at this show
write bad jokes,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.869
Shoemaker shoves them
out the window.

00:05:03.869 --> 00:05:06.172
I referred to it as a fable
and was told

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:07.540
it's not a fable,
it's a fairy tale.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.008
Fables have to have a moral.

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:14.080
But I think if you asked any of
the elves who have written here,

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.847
they would say
the moral of the story is

00:05:15.847 --> 00:05:18.217
steer clear of Shoemaker.

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:23.055
Someone also said

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.490
"You shouldn't say
'throw out the window,'

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.828
you should say 'defenestrate.'"

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.530
Defenestrate means
to throw out the window.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.400
Which I believe,
but if anyone ever

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.802
uses it in a sentence
around you,

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:38.304
you have my permission to

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.473
throw them out
the [bleep] window.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:43.409
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:46.412
This is one of the fun things
about doing "Corrections,"

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.781
is you sometimes do some digging
on words.

00:05:49.781 --> 00:05:51.784
Defenestrate --

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:54.186
I looked it up, and they say

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:57.523
the most famous example
of defenestration

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:03.095
was in 1618,
when two imperial regents

00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:08.134
were thrown out the window
of the Prague Castle,

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:12.738
an event that started
the Thirty Year War.

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:14.207
Here's the most
fascinating thing.

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:18.677
Neither of the guys who were
thrown out of the window died.

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:21.880
They landed in a moat.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:24.183
And I just feel like

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:28.620
if you're going to start
a 30-year war over it,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.723
they should have died.

00:06:31.723 --> 00:06:33.592
I just feel like -- I feel bad
for the soldier

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:36.095
like 17 years in who was like,
"Why are we doing this again?"

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:38.130
And they were like, "Iunno.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.065
They threw Vilem and Jakub
out the window."

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:41.834
And he's like, "Oh, yeah,
that's a hell of a way to go."

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.903
And it's like,
"Oh, no, they didn't die."

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.772
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:52.178
Interesting fact about
the Thirty Year War --

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:55.081
named at the beginning.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:57.583
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:58.918
They said -- I guess they said,

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:02.655
"Alright, we're going
to give this 30 years.

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:04.990
We obviously have
a lot of disagreements,

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.728
but if we can't sort it out
after 30 years, we all go home."

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:16.635
This bummed me out
because we're writing jokes,

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:20.539
and we wrote a joke about guys
from New Jersey

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:22.575
settling New Jersey,

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:25.043
and they were kind of
like Sopranos-y guys.

00:07:25.043 --> 00:07:29.415
And I pitched the joke,
"Hey, look at this farmland.

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:32.585
Let's plant some gabagool,"
and a bunch of people here --

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:35.654
oh, you jackals will be thrilled
to know how many people said,

00:07:35.654 --> 00:07:37.156
"Well, you don't plant
gabagool."

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:40.726
And I'm like, "I know that.
That's the joke."

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.395
And they said, "You're going
to hear it from the jackals."

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:44.430
And guess what --
I heard it from the jackals.

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:50.502
I'm a little...

00:07:50.502 --> 00:07:52.771
I'm a little
under the weather.

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:55.374
I feel like now that kids
are going back to school,

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:57.910
they come home with colds.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.345
But I will say that
when I am under the weather,

00:08:00.345 --> 00:08:06.118
I have long known a sort of
homeopathic cure

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:10.456
from my friend Andy Samberg,
so let's see if he's there.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.790
[ Line ringing ]

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.292
This is his homeopathic cure,
if he answers.

00:08:14.292 --> 00:08:16.329
[ Ringing continues ]

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:25.037
Hey, do Robert Smith.

00:08:27.707 --> 00:08:31.376
-♪ Boyyyys don't cryyyy ♪

00:08:31.376 --> 00:08:33.412
-[ Laughs ] Thanks.

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:35.281
[ Laughter ]

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:44.423
That's The Cure.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.630
Sometimes you just, you know,
you wake up on Friday morning,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.099
you start scrolling through
the corrections on "Corrections"

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:56.434
and you're just gutted
by an error you made.

00:08:56.434 --> 00:08:57.836
I've lived my entire life

00:08:57.836 --> 00:08:59.672
thinking that David
and Richard Attenborough

00:08:59.672 --> 00:09:02.007
were one person.

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:04.376
They're two different people.

00:09:04.376 --> 00:09:06.245
David Attenborough
is the naturalist

00:09:06.245 --> 00:09:07.680
and Richard Attenborough
is his brother,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.416
who was in "Jurassic Park,"
"The Great Escape."

00:09:11.416 --> 00:09:13.218
Just thought they were
the same person.

00:09:13.218 --> 00:09:15.054
Ashamed.

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:18.257
Must have been
quite a sibling rivalry

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:22.294
between a man
who committed his life

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:24.964
to covering the natural world
in all its wonder

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:28.100
and his brother
who made a dinosaur movie.

00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:32.038
He must've been so happy
to tell him

00:09:32.038 --> 00:09:34.373
he was making a big-budget movie

00:09:34.373 --> 00:09:35.941
about dinosaurs.

00:09:35.941 --> 00:09:37.943
He's like...
[Cockney accent] "Oi, David" --

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:39.678
I'm assuming.

00:09:39.678 --> 00:09:41.347
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.347 --> 00:09:43.049
I mean, they have fancy voices

00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:44.249
for like, you know,
their narration

00:09:44.249 --> 00:09:46.652
and when they do films,
but it's like,

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:51.624
"Oi, David, did Mum tell you
I'm doing a new film?"

00:09:51.624 --> 00:09:53.759
He's like, "Oh, wassat, Dickie?"

00:09:56.162 --> 00:09:59.231
He said, "Yeah, it's about a
bunch of people on an island.

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:00.900
Some very interesting wildlife."

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.836
And he's like," Oh, you shoulda
asked me then, Dickie.

00:10:04.836 --> 00:10:06.338
I know quite a bit
about wildlife."

00:10:06.338 --> 00:10:07.672
He's like, "No, I don't think
you know about this.

00:10:07.672 --> 00:10:09.775
It's about dinosaurs."

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:12.944
[ Laughter ]

00:10:12.944 --> 00:10:17.082
"Yeah, we don't want to do
a show about otters.

00:10:17.082 --> 00:10:19.151
Newts."

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:21.187
[ Laughter ]

00:10:23.655 --> 00:10:27.792
We showed a photo of Notre Dame

00:10:27.792 --> 00:10:29.795
because the Catholic --
the joke was the Catholic Church

00:10:29.795 --> 00:10:31.863
was going to have to
auction off the Notre Dame.

00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:35.301
And we did a Zillow ad.
Can we show that real quick?

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:42.508
And a bunch of people said
that the spire collapsed.

00:10:42.508 --> 00:10:44.610
And they're right.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:49.014
I would say the spire
famously collapsed.

00:10:49.014 --> 00:10:50.850
I feel like as far as spire --

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.752
top-three spire collapses
in the last decade?

00:10:53.752 --> 00:10:55.187
[ Laughter ]

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:00.292
Some people think
we are doing this

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:01.761
for the full audience.

00:11:01.761 --> 00:11:04.763
I assure you that we're not.

00:11:04.763 --> 00:11:07.532
But, you know, more people
are coming to "Corrections."

00:11:07.532 --> 00:11:11.036
I mean, it's all crew and staff,
but it's a hot ticket.

00:11:11.036 --> 00:11:14.373
And people who come to
the show are gutted

00:11:14.373 --> 00:11:16.875
that they are not
allowed to stay.

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:18.710
Because I think
they come on Thursday

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.513
assuming they get to stay
for "Corrections,"

00:11:21.513 --> 00:11:23.516
which they don't,
and it was hard because some --

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:26.685
their disappointment
is sometimes palpable.

00:11:26.685 --> 00:11:28.487
Like tonight,
Taylor Swift was our guest,

00:11:28.487 --> 00:11:31.190
and she walked out
and it was just silence.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.192
[ Laughter ]

00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:35.561
Because they were like,
"Where's 'Corrections'?"

00:11:35.561 --> 00:11:37.096
[ Laughter ]

00:11:41.333 --> 00:11:43.802
This is -- I don't want to be...

00:11:43.802 --> 00:11:46.072
[ Inhales sharply ]
I don't know.

00:11:46.072 --> 00:11:47.205
[ Sighs ]

00:11:47.205 --> 00:11:49.241
I don't want to,
like, tsk-tsk...

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:56.849
...blue humor, but someone
wrote a filthy joke.

00:11:56.849 --> 00:11:59.551
And we kind of -- I hate to be

00:11:59.551 --> 00:12:02.454
the police of the "Corrections"
comment section,

00:12:02.454 --> 00:12:04.323
but it's just, we have
to be better than that.

00:12:04.323 --> 00:12:07.392
We did a joke about

00:12:07.392 --> 00:12:10.762
a grandfather telling his kids
how when he was younger,

00:12:10.762 --> 00:12:14.266
he took a date to see "Ben-Hur"

00:12:14.266 --> 00:12:17.335
and he took -- you know,
he and the date --

00:12:17.335 --> 00:12:19.137
you know, obviously we implied.

00:12:19.137 --> 00:12:20.639
We did not use filthy language.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:24.609
We implied that they maybe,
you know, hooked up

00:12:24.609 --> 00:12:27.979
or whatever
they called it back then.

00:12:27.979 --> 00:12:31.416
And someone wrote --
this was their joke.

00:12:31.416 --> 00:12:32.651
This is the first
half of the joke,

00:12:32.651 --> 00:12:34.320
because the second half
is just too filthy.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.256
"Was Grandpa's date annoyed

00:12:37.256 --> 00:12:42.494
he went down on her
during 'Ben-Hur'?"

00:12:42.494 --> 00:12:47.066
And this is not the kind
of language we use here.

00:12:47.066 --> 00:12:50.102
And if you think I'm going to,
like, read a punch line

00:12:50.102 --> 00:12:52.971
to a joke,
"Was Grandpa's date annoyed

00:12:52.971 --> 00:12:56.241
he went down on her
during 'Ben-Hur'?"

00:12:56.241 --> 00:12:58.277
I mean, in what world...

00:13:00.178 --> 00:13:03.548
...would I share that

00:13:03.548 --> 00:13:07.719
with the people who spend time
with me here?

00:13:07.719 --> 00:13:10.556
I mean, shame on you.

00:13:10.556 --> 00:13:12.324
[ Laughter ]

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:15.394
See me next week.

